1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,600
That night, at two in the morning, Aziz's phone buzzes.

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:05,472
A code from the bank.

3
00:00:05,522 --> 00:00:07,344
Then another one.

4
00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,216
He did not ask for any of them.

5
00:00:09,266 --> 00:00:15,000
Somewhere, a script calls the registration of the mobile bank with his number, again and again.

6
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:21,608
Who stops it, and who stops the next step, when the script starts to guess the code?

7
00:00:21,658 --> 00:00:29,168
That afternoon, Aziz registered with a code like these, and the platform kept only a fingerprint of it, as Lesson 8 showed.

8
00:00:29,218 --> 00:00:31,880
Tonight a stranger asks for the same kind of code.

9
00:00:31,930 --> 00:00:40,928
This lesson shows the rules around it: how long it lives, how many tries it gets, and how many codes one number, one address or one device can ask for.

10
00:00:40,978 --> 00:00:42,800
Start with the code itself.

11
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:47,096
It is random, with enough digits that a guess is very unlikely.

12
00:00:47,146 --> 00:00:50,744
The platform keeps it in a small table of codes in Postgres.

13
00:00:50,794 --> 00:00:52,616
It does not keep the code.

14
00:00:52,666 --> 00:00:59,288
It keeps a keyed digest, made with the code pepper, a secret that lives in Vault, as Lesson 6 showed.

15
00:00:59,338 --> 00:01:02,144
So a copy of the database gives nobody a code.

16
00:01:02,194 --> 00:01:06,128
Each new code also makes the older codes of the same journey void.

17
00:01:06,178 --> 00:01:09,104
So at any moment, one code is alive.

18
00:01:09,154 --> 00:01:15,392
The code is never in clear at rest, not even after it goes to TAF, and it never goes to a log.

19
00:01:15,442 --> 00:01:20,240
When Aziz types a code, the platform compares the digests in constant time.

20
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:26,384
A wrong code and a nearly right code take the same time to answer, so the timing tells an attacker nothing.

21
00:01:26,434 --> 00:01:28,256
Now the limits on one code.

22
00:01:28,306 --> 00:01:30,680
Each is a policy that the bank sets.

23
00:01:30,730 --> 00:01:32,552
The code has a short lifetime.

24
00:01:32,602 --> 00:01:35,216
It accepts only a few wrong entries.

25
00:01:35,266 --> 00:01:39,536
Then the answer is: wrong code, and the number of tries that remain.

26
00:01:39,586 --> 00:01:45,512
After the last wrong entry, the code is void, and the person must ask for a new one.

27
00:01:45,562 --> 00:01:52,784
A new code needs a minimum interval since the last one: ask too soon, and the answer is wait, then ask again.

28
00:01:52,834 --> 00:01:55,592
And each journey gets only a few resends.

29
00:01:55,642 --> 00:01:57,584
One more limit comes from outside.

30
00:01:57,634 --> 00:02:03,320
TAF has its own lock: after three wrong codes, it blocks the number for ten minutes.

31
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:07,160
So the platform's limit on wrong entries is never more than three.

32
00:02:07,210 --> 00:02:12,248
With a higher limit, TAF would lock Aziz out before the platform even counts.

33
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:14,696
So guessing one code does not work.

34
00:02:14,746 --> 00:02:17,600
But the script at two in the morning does not guess.

35
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:19,520
It simply asks for new codes.

36
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:21,464
That is where the rate limits come in.

37
00:02:21,514 --> 00:02:25,832
The platform counts three things, in the time zone of the installation.

38
00:02:25,882 --> 00:02:28,592
The code requests for each phone number in one day.

39
00:02:28,642 --> 00:02:31,928
The code requests for each network address in one hour.

40
00:02:31,978 --> 00:02:35,192
And the registrations for each device in one day.

41
00:02:35,242 --> 00:02:37,160
The bank sets each limit.

42
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:42,416
A fourth counter watches the whole installation: all the code messages in one hour.

43
00:02:42,466 --> 00:02:45,536
At its alert level, operations get an alert.

44
00:02:45,586 --> 00:02:48,104
The script hits the limit for Aziz's number.

45
00:02:48,154 --> 00:02:52,472
Its next request gets one answer: too many requests, try later.

46
00:02:52,522 --> 00:02:57,632
The platform writes an audit event, and sends an event to the bank's antifraud system.

47
00:02:57,682 --> 00:02:59,576
Aziz's phone goes quiet.

48
00:02:59,626 --> 00:03:01,976
These counters live in Valkey.

49
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:09,680
A counter there can carry its own expiry, so a cool-down, the wait before the next try, simply runs out by itself.

50
00:03:09,730 --> 00:03:13,568
And a read takes less than a millisecond, even at peak load.

51
00:03:13,618 --> 00:03:18,608
The wrong entries of each code are a counter there too, and that counter ends with the code.

52
00:03:18,658 --> 00:03:21,440
And nothing in Valkey is the only copy.

53
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:25,760
Each value there either comes again from Postgres, or expires.

54
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:28,976
The most important rule in this lesson is a short one.

55
00:03:29,026 --> 00:03:31,760
A limit in the app is only for convenience.

56
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:35,048
The app can grey out a button, but only the server counts.

57
00:03:35,098 --> 00:03:37,808
The script at two in the morning never opened the app.

58
00:03:37,858 --> 00:03:42,128
It called the API directly, and the API still said no.

59
00:03:42,178 --> 00:03:46,016
The same idea guards a much more expensive call: MyID.

60
00:03:46,066 --> 00:03:49,448
Lesson 13 shows its own counters and cool-downs.

61
00:03:49,498 --> 00:03:55,880
Now suppose the script slows down: one request each hour, each time from a new network address.

62
00:03:55,930 --> 00:03:59,440
Which counter still stops it?

63
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:06,112
The counter for his phone number, because it counts every code request for that number in one day, from any address.

64
00:04:06,162 --> 00:04:11,728
One code, kept as a keyed digest, with a short life and at most three wrong entries.

65
00:04:11,778 --> 00:04:17,224
Rate limits for the number, the address and the device, and an alert for the whole installation.

66
00:04:17,274 --> 00:04:22,120
Counters and cool-downs in Valkey, and every decision on the server.

67
00:04:22,170 --> 00:04:27,232
The code that Aziz typed on Sunday was right, but a right code is not the whole story.

68
00:04:27,282 --> 00:04:34,432
TAF also checks where it arrived, and Lesson 10 shows that verdict, on a Monday morning in a café.
